new_policy:
A Large Language Model (LLM), chatbot, or other AI cannot be credited as an author, as authorship requires that the author be accountable for the submitted/published work, and artificial intelligence cannot fulfil this requirement of authorship;
Authors listed on the paper must review the content generated by the LLM and take full responsibility for it, as they would for any other content within the submitted/published work;
The use of LLM tools must be documented in the Methods, Acknowledgments, or another appropriate section of the paper.

A Large Language Model (LLM), chatbot, or other AI cannot be credited as an author, as authorship requires that the author be accountable for the submitted/published work, and artificial intelligence cannot fulfil this requirement of authorship;
Authors listed on the paper must review the content generated by the LLM and take full responsibility for it, as they would for any other content within the submitted/published work;
The use of LLM tools must be documented in the Methods, Acknowledgments, or another appropriate section of the paper.

"Can Artificial Intelligence Be Considered An Author?
Current law recognizes that copyright protection extends only to human-created content. According to the US Copyright Office, copyright does not extend to purely artificial intelligence (AI)-generated material, or to material where there is insufficient human control over the expressive elements. AI technology products do not qualify for authorship under SLACK Journals editorial policy. Therefore, authors should not list AI or AI-assisted technologies as an author or coauthor. This policy is in line with the Committee on Publication Ethics (COPE) position statement on the use of AI and AI-assisted technology in manuscript preparation, which states: “authors are fully responsible for the content of their manuscript, even those parts produced by an AI tool, and are thus liable for any breach of publication ethics.”

Use of nonhuman artificial intelligence (AI), large language models (LLMs), machine learning, or similar technologies is permitted, and disclosure is NOT required, to improve the readability and language of the manuscript under the oversight and control of the authors in the following ways:

To check grammar, spelling, references, and similar (eg, Grammarly, QuillBot, Edifix);
To refine or format the presentation of research data reported in the manuscript (eg, as supplementary material); and
To assist with the compilation of reference lists. Suggested references must be accessible in full text to the author, and the author must confirm their relevance to the manuscript topic.
Use of AI technologies are permitted, and disclosure IS required, when:

formulating questions or hypotheses;
designing and conducting experiments;
drafting parts of the paper;
translating parts or the entire draft of the original manuscript into a secondary language;
summarizing, paraphrasing, significantly revising, or synthesizing textual content;
collecting, analyzing, interpreting, or visualizing data (quantitative or qualitative);
extracting data for review of the literature (systematic or not);
identifying knowledge gaps; and
generating synthetic data and images reported in the paper or used in research.
Authors are NOT permitted to use AI tools to create or assist with creating or editing entire manuscripts except as mentioned above, to generate entire sections of text (with the exception of translations), or to generate images, graphs, or tables.

